---
// Install, as a doc: the ISO first, because a fresh install is the common
// case; the key enrollment the NVIDIA image needs; the switch for a machine
// already on an atomic Fedora; and what the first login and updates are like.
// The home page's #install is the short version and links here for the rest.
import Iso from '../../components/Iso.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, IMAGES, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'Install Pulsar from the weekly ISO, or switch to it from Fedora Silverblue or Kinoite with one command. How to enroll the NVIDIA key, and how updates work.';

const ENROL = 'sudo mokutil --import /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der\nsudo systemctl reboot';
const TAKE = `mokutil --test-key /etc/pki/pulsar/MOK.der   # ...is already enrolled\nsudo bootc switch ${IMAGES.nvidia}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const CHECK = 'modinfo -F signer nvidia\nnvidia-smi';
const switchTo = (image: string) => `sudo bootc switch \\\n  ${image}:latest\nsudo systemctl reboot`;
const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Install Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/install', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const MOK = ['Enroll MOK', 'View key 0', 'Continue', 'Yes', 'password', 'reboot'];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'iso', label: 'A fresh install', level: 2 },
  { id: 'secure-boot', label: 'NVIDIA: enroll the key, once', level: 2 },
  { id: 'switch', label: 'Switch from an atomic Fedora', level: 2 },
  { id: 'first-login', label: 'When you log in', level: 2 },
  { id: 'updates', label: 'Updates', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Install"
  fullTitle="Install Pulsar — the weekly ISO, or one bootc switch"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/install"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    A new machine: one download and a USB stick. A machine on Silverblue, Kinoite or another atomic
    Fedora: one command.
  </p>

  <h2 id="iso">A fresh install</h2>
  <Iso />
  <p class="hint">
    From Windows or a Mac, and keeping Windows: <a href="/docs/coming-from">From Windows or macOS</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Boot from the stick and start the installer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You choose the disk and partitioning.</strong> Nothing is erased until you confirm;
      back up first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Account and Wi-Fi:</strong> the installer’s accounts step, or GNOME’s setup on first
      boot.
    </li>
    <li>It waits on its last screen. Remove the stick and restart.</li>
  </ul>
  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Use the current ISO, not an old download">
    ISOs from early August 2026 erased every disk they could see (under Ventoy, the stick too)
    without asking. The <code>-latest</code> links above are always the current, safe build.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="secure-boot">NVIDIA: enroll the key, once</h2>
  <p>
    The NVIDIA driver is signed with Pulsar’s key, so Secure&nbsp;Boot stays on. Enroll the key
    once, through MokManager on the next boot. The standard image doesn’t need this.
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Import the certificate</h3>
      <p><code>mokutil</code> asks for a one-time password you retype at the next boot.</p>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="enroll" code={ENROL}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Answer MokManager</h3>
      <p>The next boot stops in a blue firmware screen. The sequence is:</p>
      <p class="mok">
        {MOK.map((step, index) => (
          <>
            <arc-kbd>{step}</arc-kbd>
            {index < MOK.length - 1 && <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>}
          </>
        ))}
      </p>
      <p class="hint">Missed it? Nothing breaks; run the import again.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Switching, not installing? Take the NVIDIA image</h3>
      <p>Installed from the NVIDIA ISO? Skip this. Otherwise, once the key is in:</p>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="switch" code={TAKE}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Check the driver loaded</h3>
      <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="check" code={CHECK}></arc-code-block>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <arc-alert variant="warning" heading="Lost the key later?">
    A BIOS update can clear the MOK list. Accept GNOME Software’s Secure Boot prompt: it enrolls{' '}
    <code>/etc/pki/akmods/certs/public_key.der</code>, which Pulsar keeps identical to{' '}
    <code>MOK.der</code>. Or run the import above again.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="switch">Switch from an atomic Fedora</h2>
  <p>
    From Silverblue, Kinoite or another atomic Fedora 44+, switch in place. Files and accounts are
    kept; the old image stays in the boot menu until the next update.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="standard" code={switchTo(IMAGES.vanilla)}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="nvidia" code={switchTo(IMAGES.nvidia)}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    NVIDIA with Secure Boot on: switch to the standard image first,{' '}
    <a href="#secure-boot">enroll the key</a> it ships, then switch to NVIDIA.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first-login">When you log in</h2>
  <p>
    New accounts start in Pulsar’s theme with a welcome window: pick a look, learn the basics,
    optionally add a coding agent. Reopen it from <strong>Welcome to Pulsar</strong> in the app
    grid. A customized account you switched keeps its look.
  </p>
  <p>
    Default apps (Steam, Heroic, video and music players, the GNOME set and more) install on the
    first boot with a network. <code>sudo pulsar setup apps</code> reinstalls any that are missing.
  </p>
  <p>If something looks wrong:</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="health" code="pulsar doctor"></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    <a href="/docs/troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/docs/where-things-go">Where things go</a>
  </p>

  <h2 id="updates">Updates</h2>
  <p>
    New builds download in the background (mains power, unmetered connection) and apply at your
    next restart; a notification offers a Restart button. <code>sudo pulsar update</code> downloads
    on demand; <code>sudo pulsar rollback</code> returns to the previous build.{' '}
    <a href="/docs/updates">Updates and rollback</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 var(--space-xl);
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xl);
    counter-reset: step;
  }
  .steps > li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 3rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .steps > li::before {
    counter-increment: step;
    content: counter(step);
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    text-shadow: var(--glow-xs);
    line-height: var(--heading-lh);
  }
  .steps h3 {
    margin-top: 0 !important;
  }
  .mok {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .arrow {
    color: var(--text-ghost);
  }
</style>
